  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Verifying Module Versions**\n\nI\'m verifying the specific version of `@google/genai` currently installed, checking in `node_modules`. Simultaneously, I\'m building out the backend components; that includes `server/openMeteo.ts`, `server/geminiBriefing.ts`, and `server.ts`. Finally, I\'ll be updating the `package.json` scripts, to properly integrate the new code.\n\n\n**Checking API Integrations**\n\nI\'m now integrating the Open-Meteo API. I\'m building out `server/openMeteo.ts` to use their Geocoding and Air Quality APIs, ensuring correct URL formatting. Simultaneously, I\'m working on backend components: `server/geminiBriefing.ts` and `server.ts`. I am running this in parallel with updating the `package.json` scripts, to be able to use `dev` and `start`. I am also verifying the `@google/genai` version, to make sure it is correct!\n\n\n**Analyzing API Data Structures**\n\nI\'m now examining the API responses from Open-Meteo, specifically the structure of the data it returns for weather forecasts and air quality indices. I\'m focusing on the `current` object and `hourly` arrays, to understand how the `time` strings are formatted, and how they relate to the local timezone when `timezone=auto`. My current focus is how to use these responses.\n\n\n**Defining Air Quality Metrics**\n\nI\'m now defining thresholds for air quality indicators, including `PM2.5`, `PM10`, `NO\342\202\202`, `O\342\202\203`, `SO\342\202\202`, and `CO`, drawing from WHO guidelines. I\'m also creating an algorithm to assess thermal inversion risk, using wind speed, humidity, temperature change, and PM2.5 levels to define \"high\", \"moderate\", or \"low\" risk. I\'m moving on to calculating the best activity windows.\n\n\n**Calculating Activity Windows**\n\nI\'m now calculating optimal two-hour activity windows for outdoor activities, focusing on minimizing exposure to poor air quality. For activities like walking, cross-ventilation, children\'s play, and evening errands, I\'m finding the lowest AQI windows within the given timeframes. I\'m also developing ratings based on average US AQI levels and will include protective notes. Additionally, I\'m setting up a short TTL cache to prevent Open-Meteo API spam. I am also checking `server/geminiBriefing.ts`.\n\n\n**Initializing Gemini Integration**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on initializing the Gemini integration. I\'ve set up the `GoogleGenAI` instance with the API key and a custom user agent. I\'m using the `gemini-4-hydrogen` model with a strict JSON response. I have no parameters for temperature and other settings, to keep it simple. Finally, I\'m defining a rich `DailyBriefing` response schema.\n\n\n**Refining Response Structures**\n\nI\'m now refining the `DailyBriefing` response schema. I\'m focusing on defining concrete, location-tailored recommendations. I am making sure the recommendations span all three categories: `routine`, `indoor_hack`, and `sustainable_alternative`. Each should have an `id`, `category`, `title`, `effort`, `timeframe`, `steps`, `directHealthImpact`, `directEmissionImpact`, `scientificMechanism`, `estimatedCo2KgSaved` and `estimatedExposureDropPct`. I\'ll propagate any Gemini error, with a clear message.\n\n\n"
