  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Diagnosing Location Data**\n\nI\'m now diving into the source code to pinpoint the cause of the New Delhi data appearing when Kolkata is requested. Simultaneously, I\'m sketching out the best architecture to quickly generate today\'s briefing and tailored routines, aiming for a system that\'s both flexible and speedy.\n\n\n**Investigating Source Code**\n\nI\'m deep in the code now, specifically targeting the location data issue. I\'ve identified potential race conditions in `src/App.tsx`, particularly around the `fetchAir` function and how it interacts with user location selection. A stale Delhi response could be overwriting Kolkata\'s data if the requests aren\'t handled carefully, and I\'m looking at implementing request IDs or AbortControllers to prevent this. I\'m also examining the reverse geocoding within the live location logic for other clues.\n\n\n**Analyzing Server Response**\n\nI\'ve shifted my focus to `server.ts` to understand how latitude and longitude are handled. I\'m investigating how the server processes coordinates, particularly when `navigator.geolocation` fails, is blocked, or returns potentially invalid data. I need to understand why New Delhi data might be served when Kolkata is requested, even if the coordinates are passed through the request. I\'m focusing on the logic around `hasCoords`, and the use of hardcoded lat/lon values to see if they\'re playing a role. I\'m going to run two key tests with Open-Meteo: comparing the responses for Kolkata and New Delhi, and scrutinizing `handleUseGeolocation`\342\200\231s behavior.\n\n\n**Refining Geolocation Logic**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical flaw: when \"Use my location\" is clicked, if reverse geocoding fails or isn\'t attempted, and `navigator.geolocation` fails or is blocked, the UI can incorrectly show New Delhi data instead of an error. I\'m focusing on `handleUseGeolocation`, specifically how it deals with successful Kolkata coordinates from the API, iframe permissions, and potential IP geolocation fallbacks. I need to ensure that regardless of the source, we always reverse-geocode the location to display the actual city name on the UI.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Stale Data**\n\nI\'ve homed in on the root cause: the `airLoading` state in `src/App.tsx` allows stale data, specifically New Delhi, to persist while Kolkata\'s data is fetched. This results in the incorrect display. Additionally, I need to address `ForecastChart.tsx`, which retains the active index even on location changes, although this is a minor issue. I\'m scrutinizing the API response at `/api/air-data?lat=22.5726&lon=88.3`. I am exploring aborting previous requests to correct this.\n\n\n"
