  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Performance Issues**\n\nI\'ve hit a major snag! A `curl` call to `/api/briefing` took over 10 seconds and went background. The culprit seems to be the default `ThinkingLevel.HIGH` setting in the `gemini-api` Skill, lines 498-506 of `SKILL.md`. This is a significant performance bottleneck that needs immediate attention.\n\n\n**Analyzing Root Cause**\n\nI\'ve discovered the root cause of the performance issue and a crucial location data discrepancy! The `gemini-4-hydrogen` model was running with excessive `ThinkingLevel.HIGH` tokens due to a missing configuration setting, and a separate issue is causing Kolkata\'s live location to display New Delhi\'s data. I\'ve located the exact lines of code in `server.ts` and `server/openMeteo.ts` to examine.\n\n\n**Examining Geolocation Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating the geolocation flow, specifically how the location data is handled when a user clicks \"Use my location.\" It looks like the `handleUseGeolocation` function in `src/App.tsx` captures the user\'s coordinates. However, I\'m concerned that the `country` field uses a coordinate string. This isn\'t ideal! I need to determine how this coordinate string is passed to `fetchAir` and if there might be a conversion issue or if other parts of the system are expecting a name or ID.\n\n\n**Uncovering Critical Bug**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed a major bug related to how location updates are handled. When the location changes from \"New Delhi\" to \"Current Location,\" the `AirOverviewHero` component continues displaying \"New Delhi\" air data while a new `air-data` request for \"Current Location\" is already fetching. Crucially, the old, lengthy `/api/briefing` request for New Delhi can overwrite the Kolkata (or current location) briefing data because there\'s no abort controller. This asynchronous timing vulnerability leads to incorrect information being displayed.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Race Condition**\n\nI\'ve uncovered a critical race condition. After the initial Kolkata data loads, a lingering New Delhi briefing call overwrites it with incorrect details like the briefing summary and alerts. This happens because `/api/briefing` calls aren\'t aborted, causing old data to supersede the current location\'s data. I\'m investigating how cached geolocation data and the distinction between `current`, `hourly`, and `airCache` in `server/openMeteo.ts` might contribute, along with potential issues stemming from direct coordinate use. I will also check `LocationToolbar.tsx` to understand the flow.\n\n\n**Refining Geolocation Strategy**\n\nI\'m now refining the geolocation strategy to improve accuracy and robustness. I\'ve considered three cases for `navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition`. Case A: Browser geolocation returns coordinates, so I immediately call `/api/reverse-geocode` on the server to get a precise location string. Case B: I\'m ensuring high accuracy with a defined timeout, using `enableHighAccuracy: true` and a shorter `maximumAge: 0` to get fresh data instead of cached data. Case C: I\'m considering scenarios with manual searches or clicks.\n\n\n**Implementing Abort Strategy**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `AbortController` and monotonically increasing request ID strategy in `App.tsx`. The goal is to ensure that any delayed requests from previous locations, like New Delhi, are immediately aborted and ignored when a new location is selected. I also plan to clear the snapshot or display a clear switching indicator, and clear stale briefing information, to prevent lingering New Delhi data from overwriting Kolkata\'s data. Additionally, I\'ll reset `ForecastChart`\'s `activeIndex` to show correct hourly data. Finally, I\'ll add Kolkata to `PRESET_CITIES` for easier access.\n\n\n**Optimizing API Response**\n\nI\'m now focused on significantly improving API response times. I\'m exploring using `ThinkingLevel.LOW` on `gemini-4-hydrogen`, which minimizes latency and cost and checking the speed performance of `gemini-4-hydrogen` with `ThinkingLevel.LOW` versus `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` with `ThinkingLevel.MINIMAL`. I will trim prompt verbosity, and tighten output strings. Furthermore, I will add a short server-side in-memory cache to the `briefingCache` (keyed by rounded lat/lon, current AQI bucket, profile hash, 5-minute TTL, bypassed on manual regeneration) for faster switching or reloading, making it near-instant.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "zrwBa-jQMNiMjrEPjZXyoQ0"
